A Time-based One-Time Password is the 6-digit code in an authenticator app. Server and app share a secret. Both compute HMAC(secret, current_time // 30) and cut the result down to 6 digits. RFC 6238 builds on HOTP (RFC 4226) and recommends a 30-second time step. It needs only the standard library:
# totp.py — RFC 6238 TOTP, standard library only
import base64, hashlib, hmac, secrets, struct, time
def hotp(key: bytes, counter: int, digits: int = 6) -> str:
mac = hmac.new(key, struct.pack(">Q", counter), hashlib.sha1).digest()
offset = mac[-1] & 0x0F # dynamic truncation
code = struct.unpack(">I", mac[offset:offset + 4])[0] & 0x7FFFFFFF
return str(code % 10 ** digits).zfill(digits)
def totp(key: bytes, at: float | None = None, step: int = 30, digits: int = 6) -> str:
t = time.time() if at is None else at
return hotp(key, int(t // step), digits)
def verify(key: bytes, code: str, window: int = 1) -> bool:
now = time.time() # accept one step of clock drift each way
return any(hmac.compare_digest(totp(key, now + i * 30), code)
for i in range(-window, window + 1))
if __name__ == "__main__":
rfc_key = b"12345678901234567890" # RFC 6238 Appendix B test key
print(totp(rfc_key, at=59, digits=8)) # 94287082
print(totp(rfc_key, at=1111111109, digits=8)) # 07081804
secret = base64.b32encode(secrets.token_bytes(20)).decode()
print("secret for the app:", secret) # shown to the user as a QR code
key = base64.b32decode(secret)
print("valid:", verify(key, totp(key))) # True
Real systems also remember the last accepted time step, so the same code cannot be used twice, and they rate-limit code guesses. In production you would use a tested library such as pyotp. NIST marks SMS codes as a "restricted" authenticator because of risks such as SIM swaps and number porting.

